@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import { delegation, brief text }
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import { child host }
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
    agent definition, agent entry, agent file, medium tier, skill folder,
  thinking level, tool list, model endpoint,
  parallel limit,
}
@design/claude-mcp-server.sigil from ClaudeMcpServer import { allow rule }
@design/claude-routing-hooks.sigil from ClaudeRoutingHooks import { Agent tool }
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import { store folder, parent session id }
@design/spike-harness.sigil from SpikeHarness import { pass directory, staged sigil skills }

component SigilWiring {
  goal {
    Express the experiment as files: the subzero agent for the interpreter, and
    the
    evaluator agent definitions of each experiment arm, so that arms A and B
    differ
    only in where interpreters run, with A-cheap and B-control as their
    controls
    and B-low as the control for the thinking level.
  }

  state {
    An *experiment arm* is one of five setups. A is the evaluator subagent with
    Claude interpreter subagents. A-cheap is A with the interpreter subagent on
    Haiku. B is the evaluator subagent with interpreters delegated to pi.
    B-control
    is B with the pi interpreter on the Claude model that A's interpreters use.
    B-low is B with the pi interpreter at the thinking level low.

    The *evaluator subagent* is a native Claude Code subagent that reviews a
    design
    with the sigil skills. The *interpreter agent* reads one prepared source
    and
    writes its claim rows to the *answer file* that its brief text names.

    The agent file holds three model tiers on the child host pi, the
    general agent and the fallback agent: the medium tier
    on the
    r9700/Qwen3.8 model at the thinking level high, the tier low on that model
    at
    the thinking level low, and the tier control on the Claude model of A's
    interpreters at the thinking level high. The sigil-interpreter agent entry
    runs
    on the medium tier with the sigil-understand and sigil-egglog skills and
    the tools read, grep, find, ls and write. The sigil-interpreter-low
    agent entry
    is the same on the tier low, and the sigil-interpreter-control agent entry
    is
    the same on the tier control.
  }

  constraints {
    In experiment arm B, the evaluator subagent must start every
    interpreter agent
    through a delegation and none through the Agent tool, and its definition
    must
    disallow the Agent tool.

    The *main conversation* must load no sigil skill or reference. The
    staged sigil skills stay outside every Claude discovery path, and the
    evaluator subagent is given their SKILL.md paths in its prompt, not through
    a
    skills preload.

    An interpreter agent must write its claim rows to the exact answer file its
    brief text names, in the form that sigilc ingest accepts, and must write no
    other file. Its only writing tool is write. The brief text carries this
    rule,
    nothing in subzero enforces it, and SpikeHarness reports each write outside
    the
    answer file.

    The brief text of a delegation to an interpreter agent must carry the
    request
    path, the binding path, the guidance paths, the exact answer file path and
    the
    round.

    Experiment arms A and B must use the same Claude model and effort for the
    evaluator subagent. B-control routes its delegation to
    sigil-interpreter-control, B-low routes to sigil-interpreter-low, and B
    routes
    to sigil-interpreter.

    The evaluator subagent definitions of B, B-control and B-low must be
    identical
    except for the interpreter agent they route to, and those of A and A-cheap
    except for the interpreter subagent they point to.

    The interpreter agent's tool list must drop grep and find if they fail
    under
    pi's offline mode.

    The wiring of each experiment arm must not modify the *sigil repository*.
  }

  interface {
    Provide a sigil-interpreter, a sigil-interpreter-control and a
    sigil-interpreter-low agent entry in examples/sigil/.agents/subzero.yaml,
    whose skill folder entries point at the staged sigil skills of the
    pass directory, without modifying the sigil repository. The same file holds
    an endpoints entry for the model endpoint r9700 with a parallel limit of 1,
    so
    that sigil-interpreter and sigil-interpreter-low share it.

    Provide an evaluator subagent definition for each experiment arm, and
    provide
    the interpreter agent as a Claude subagent for A and for A-cheap.
    SigilWiring
    provides the answer file of each interpreter agent by making its
    evaluator subagent name the file in the brief text, and the
    interpreter agent
    writes only that answer file.

    Provide a *README* that tells the user to copy the evaluator subagent for B
    into .claude/agents and to add an allow rule for the subzero tools first.
    It also warns that subzero does not track what a child writes, so the user
    reviews the tree with git and keeps a parent and a child, or two children,
    from writing the same files. It says that the store folder holds plain text
    and ignores itself, that subzero does not edit the user's own .gitignore
    and leaves an existing .gitignore in the store folder unchanged, and that
    two Claude sessions started in one project at the same time may mix the
    tasks listed for their parent session id. It also says that a
    parallel limit holds only among the child runs of one process, so that a
    terminal command or a second session is not counted, and that a
    model endpoint with no entry, such as a Claude model, has no
    parallel limit. It says that the command line has no cancel, so that a task
    started with subzero run ends only by Ctrl-C in its terminal and the server
    reports it as running in another process.
  }

  decisions {
    The evaluator is a Claude subagent and only its interpreters go to pi, so
    that
    Claude keeps the judgment and the local model gets the narrow repeated job.
    Delegation inside subzero itself moves to a later version. B-low is a
    separate
    agent definition and not a setting on the delegation, so that the
    thinking level comes from files and not from what the evaluator model
    chooses
    to pass.
  }
}
